### Musculoskeletal System and Soft Tissue - Summary

**Metabolic Bone Diseases:** Osteoporosis shows reduced trabecular bone volume with normal mineralization. Osteomalacia features thick unmineralized osteoid seams. Osteitis fibrosa cystica (hyperparathyroidism) shows increased osteoclasts and brown tumor formation. Paget disease features mosaic bone pattern with jigsaw puzzle cement lines and giant osteoclasts.

**Bone Infections:** Acute osteomyelitis shows neutrophilic inflammation with bone necrosis. Chronic phase features sequestrum (dead bone) and involucrum (reactive new bone).

**Benign Bone Tumors:** Osteoid osteoma is a small painful nidus with surrounding reactive sclerosis. Osteochondroma shows a cartilage-capped bony excrescence continuous with the medullary cavity. Enchondroma is a well-circumscribed intramedullary hyaline cartilage nodule. Giant cell tumor features uniformly distributed multinucleated giant cells in an epiphyseal location.

**Malignant Bone Tumors:** Osteosarcoma requires malignant osteoid production for diagnosis; shows pleomorphic mesenchymal cells with atypical mitoses. Ewing sarcoma presents as a small round blue cell tumor with PAS-positive glycogen and Homer-Wright pseudorosettes.

**Soft Tissue Tumors:** Lipoma is the most common benign soft tissue tumor (uniform adipocytes without lipoblasts). Liposarcoma requires lipoblasts for diagnosis. Nodular fasciitis is a reactive "tissue culture-like" spindle cell proliferation. Desmoid tumor is locally aggressive but does not metastasize. Synovial sarcoma may be monophasic or biphasic with characteristic translocation. Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma is a diagnosis of exclusion showing extreme pleomorphism.
